:: StoryTitle
Literature Review Scenario

:: Start
You are beginning a literature review for a public health module.

Your task is to move from a broad topic to a defensible review question.

[[Define the question first->Focused Question]]
[[Start searching immediately->Search Too Soon]]

:: Focused Question
You decide to clarify the review question before searching.

A focused question will help you identify relevant evidence.

<iframe width="720" height="405" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/yt3e8Ng0mf0" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen></iframe>

<iframe width="720" height="405" src="https://lshtm.cloud.panopto.eu/Panopto/Pages/Embed.aspx?id=d19ba573-9ad1-480b-95db-b3ed01014aab" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen></iframe>

[[Develop a search strategy->Search Strategy]]

:: Search Too Soon
You begin searching immediately and find many inconsistent papers.

The topic is still too broad.

[[Go back and define the question->Focused Question]]

:: Search Strategy
You identify key concepts, keywords and possible databases.

[[Search PubMed and Scopus->Evidence Search]]

:: Evidence Search
You locate a collection of potentially relevant studies.

[[Screen titles and abstracts->Screening]]

:: Screening
Some studies are clearly relevant while others should be excluded.

[[Apply inclusion and exclusion criteria->Critical Appraisal]]

:: Critical Appraisal
You assess study quality, bias and methodological rigour.

[[Synthesize findings->Synthesis]]

:: Synthesis
You identify themes, patterns and gaps across the evidence.

[[Write conclusions->Conclusion]]

:: Conclusion
You produce a transparent and evidence-informed literature review.

End of scenario.
